Source: sudosrv
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Paul Kilar <pkilar@gmail.com>
# debhelper-compat replaces the debian/compat file, which was still present
# alongside it. golang-go's floor was 2:1.26, which trixie cannot satisfy
# (it ships 1.24), so dpkg-checkbuilddeps refused the build outright. The module
# needs Go 1.27; the floor below is the version that understands GOTOOLCHAIN,
# and debian/rules sets GOTOOLCHAIN=auto so the distro toolchain fetches the
# one it needs. That requires network access at build time -- a sealed builder
# must vendor a toolchain instead.
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13),
               golang-go (>= 2:1.21~),
               protobuf-compiler
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Homepage: https://github.com/pkilar/sudosrv
Vcs-Git: https://github.com/pkilar/sudosrv.git
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: logsh
Architecture: any
# logsh-install.sh drives every install and removal step, so what IT needs is a
# hard dependency of this package. The RPM and PKGBUILD already declared these;
# Debian did not, which is the kind of gap that only shows up on a minimal
# image where the postinst fails and no symlink is ever created.
#
# coreutils and grep are NOT listed, unlike in the other two formats: both are
# Essential on Debian, so depending on them unversioned is an error
# (depends-on-essential-package-without-using-version) rather than diligence.
# awk is not essential, and is named by its real providers because a dependency
# on the virtual package alone is not satisfiable by apt.
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, mawk | gawk | original-awk
Recommends: sudosrv
Description: recording login shell for sudo I/O log servers
 logsh is a multi-call login shell that records a user's terminal session to a
 sudosrv log server, in the same sudoreplay-compatible format sudo produces.
 /usr/sbin/lbash and /usr/sbin/lzsh are symlinks to it; the name it is invoked
 under selects the real shell to run.
 .
 It belongs on every managed host, whereas the sudosrv daemon usually does not,
 which is why it is packaged separately. Installing it switches no account: see
 /usr/share/doc/logsh/logsh-deployment.md for the rollout and the mandatory
 break-glass drill before deploying anywhere you cannot walk up to.

Package: sudosrv
Architecture: any
# procps provides /bin/kill, which the unit's ExecReload runs. Without it
# `systemctl reload sudosrv` -- the documented way to apply a renewed TLS
# certificate without dropping connections -- fails.
Depends: ${shlibs:Depends}, ${misc:Depends}, procps
Recommends: sudo (>= 1.9.0)
Description: sudo I/O log server
 A Go-based sudo I/O log server that implements the sudo_logsrv.proto
 protocol. It serves as an alternative to sudo's native sudo_logsrvd,
 capable of receiving and processing I/O logs from sudo clients
 (version 1.9.0+).
 .
 The server supports dual-mode operation: local storage mode (saves logs
 locally) or relay mode (forwards to upstream server). It includes full
 sudoers compatibility for log path customization, TLS support for secure
 log transmission, and store-and-forward caching when upstream servers
 are unavailable.